' Accumulation of capital: class model
' Companion notes: notes/accumulation.md — full stereotype legend: notes/stereotypes.md
'
' Demonstrates that the drive to accumulate is not the capitalist's personal
' choice or a matter of policy, but a compulsion exerted on the personification
' of capital by competition among many capitals (Capital I, ch. 23–25).

@startuml accumulation
title IV. Accumulation of capital — competition as coercive law
caption Why accumulation is not a political choice: notes/accumulation.md

hide circle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam ranksep 55
skinparam nodesep 45
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<social relation>> #EDE7F6
  BackgroundColor<<historically specific>> #FFEBEE
  BackgroundColor<<personification>> #FFF8E1
}

class CapitalistCompetition <<social relation>> <<historically specific>> {
  many capitals, mutually independent
  and mutually indifferent
  exert coercive pressure on each other
}

class Capitalist <<personification>> {
  personification of capital
  (full definition: surplus-value-capital.puml)
}

class Capital <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
  value that valorizes itself
  (full definition: surplus-value-capital.puml)
}

class SurplusValue <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
  unpaid labour
  (full definition: surplus-value-capital.puml)
}

class ConstantCapital <<historically specific>> {
  value in means of production
}

class VariableCapital <<historically specific>> {
  value in labour-power
}

class AccumulationOfCapital <<historically specific>> {
  capitalisation of surplus value
  reproduction on an expanded scale
}

class Revenue <<historically specific>> {
  surplus value consumed unproductively
  the capitalist class's consumption fund
}

class SimpleReproduction <<historically specific>> {
  {abstract}
  limiting case: all surplus value spent
  as revenue; scale of capital unchanged
}

Capital *-- SurplusValue : appropriates
SurplusValue --> Revenue : part consumed\nunproductively as
SurplusValue --> AccumulationOfCapital : part capitalised as
SurplusValue ..> SimpleReproduction : if wholly consumed\n(never sustained)
AccumulationOfCapital --> ConstantCapital : expands
AccumulationOfCapital --> VariableCapital : expands
AccumulationOfCapital --> Capital : reconverted into\nadditional capital
Capitalist --> AccumulationOfCapital : executes\n(bearer, not author)
Capitalist --> Revenue : draws personal\nconsumption from
CapitalistCompetition --> Capitalist : compels accumulation\n(independent of will)
CapitalistCompetition ..> SimpleReproduction : rules out as\na lasting state

CapitalistCompetition -[hidden]down-> Capital
Capital -[hidden]right-> SurplusValue
SurplusValue -[hidden]down-> AccumulationOfCapital
AccumulationOfCapital -[hidden]right-> Revenue
Revenue -[hidden]right-> SimpleReproduction
AccumulationOfCapital -[hidden]down-> ConstantCapital
ConstantCapital -[hidden]right-> VariableCapital

note as NLaw
  **Necessity, not choice (Capital I, ch. 24)**
  "Accumulate, accumulate! That is Moses
  and the prophets!" The compulsion is not
  the capitalist's greed, morality, or a
  policy choice. Competition among many
  capitals turns capital's own immanent
  tendency to expand into an external
  coercive law acting on whoever occupies
  the role: accumulate, or be undersold,
  outcompeted, and eliminated as a capital.
end note

note bottom of SimpleReproduction
  Useful only as an analytical baseline
  (Capital I, ch. 23) to isolate what
  accumulation adds. Under real
  competition among many capitals it
  cannot persist: it collapses into
  reproduction on an expanded scale.
end note

note bottom of CapitalistCompetition
  Not a moral failing of individuals.
  "Many capitals" is itself historically
  specific: capital only ever exists as
  many, mutually repelling capitals
  (Grundrisse, "Notebook on capital").
end note

@enduml
